---
import "../styles/components/intake-form.css";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About Ethotechnics — Institute and Studio"
  description="What the Ethotechnics framework is, where it comes from, and how the open Institute and Studio practice fit together."
  publishedTime="2026-05-26T00:00:00.000Z"
>
  <section class="stack-lg">
    <p class="eyebrow">About the framework</p>
    <h1>
      Ethotechnics: governance and care infrastructure for high-stakes systems
    </h1>
    <p class="section-lead">
      Ethotechnics is a framework for designing, evaluating, and governing
      high-stakes socio-technical systems with explicit accountability, burden
      management, and care-infrastructure commitments.
    </p>
    <p>
      The framework is authored by <strong>Kanav Jain</strong>, and developed
      through standards writing, glossary curation, field diagnostics, and
      implementation-oriented research.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-sm card">
    <p class="eyebrow">Institute and Studio</p>
    <h2>How the Institute and Studio fit together</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Ethotechnics Institute</strong> is the public, open-access commons:
      standards, ontology, glossary, diagnostics, and field notes are free to use,
      cite, and extend.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Ethotechnics Studio</strong> provides clinical AI safety evaluations,
      VC diligence, and governance advisory through commissioned engagements. The
      Institute publishes the open framework; the Studio applies it to specific systems
      and organizations.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a
        href="https://ethotechnics.com/studio/"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        target="_blank"
      >
        Visit Ethotechnics Studio →
      </a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-sm card">
    <p class="eyebrow">What existing standards don't cover</p>
    <h2>The gap Ethotechnics addresses</h2>
    <p>
      NIST AI RMF tells you to manage risk. EU AI Act tells you to document
      compliance. ISO/IEC 42001 tells you to build a management system. OECD AI
      Principles tell you to be responsible. None of them tell you whether your
      system can actually be stopped when it's harming someone.
    </p>
    <p>
      Existing frameworks govern by representation: documents, roles, reviews,
      and attestations. Ethotechnics governs by control: stop rules, binding
      clocks, rollback authority, and contestability that changes state.
    </p>
    <table class="mapping-table mapping-table--compare">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Existing standard says</th>
          <th scope="col">Ethotechnics requires</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td
            >"Maintain human oversight" <span class="small muted"
              >(EU AI Act, Art. 14)</span
            ></td
          >
          <td
            >Named human with stop authority, tested halt path, recovery clock</td
          >
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td
            >"Manage risks across the AI lifecycle" <span class="small muted"
              >(NIST AI RMF)</span
            ></td
          >
          <td>Measurable time-in-harm bounds, exercised rollback paths</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td
            >"Conduct conformity assessment" <span class="small muted"
              >(ISO/IEC 42001)</span
            ></td
          >
          <td
            >Evidence the system can be stopped mid-incident, not just
            documented as compliant</td
          >
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td
            >"Implement responsible AI principles" <span class="small muted"
              >(OECD)</span
            ></td
          >
          <td
            >Binding escalation: owner + timer + action, or the system degrades</td
          >
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="small muted">
      Ethotechnics does not compete with these standards on whether governance
      is "important." It addresses what they leave undertreated:
      <a href="/standards/#comparison">see the full comparison →</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-sm card">
    <p class="eyebrow">The governance landscape</p>
    <h2>Where Ethotechnics fits</h2>
    <p>
      AI governance is a $440M market growing at 28% annually. Enterprises
      invest in compliance platforms, evaluation tools, and guardrail systems to
      meet emerging regulatory requirements.
    </p>
    <p>
      Ethotechnics is not a product. It is the open standard that products can
      measure against. The Institute publishes benchmarks and test cases that
      governance platforms should be evaluated against — the same way NIST
      publishes frameworks that compliance tools implement, or OWASP publishes
      standards that security tools enforce.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Institute provides the open standards. The Studio provides the
      certified evaluations.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      For commercial application (clinical AI safety evaluation, VC diligence,
      governance advisory), see
      <a
        href="https://ethotechnics.com/studio/"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        target="_blank"
      >
        Ethotechnics Studio →
      </a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-sm card">
    <p class="eyebrow">Foundations</p>
    <h2>Start with these core resources</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/glossary">Glossary</a> for core terms and definitions.</li>
      <li>
        <a href="/mechanisms">Mechanisms catalog</a> for decision logs, escalation
        patterns, and reversibility tools.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/diagnostics">Diagnostics</a> for interactive self-serve tools.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/standards">Standards</a> for formal, versioned control definitions.
      </li>
      <li><a href="/evals">Eval suites</a> for governance benchmark suites.</li>
      <li>
        <a href="/field-notes">Field notes</a> for applied essays and observations.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/quick-start">Quick start guides</a> for role-specific entry paths.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-sm card">
    <p class="eyebrow">What existing standards don't cover</p>
    <h2>The gap Ethotechnics addresses</h2>
    <p>
      NIST AI RMF tells you to manage risk. EU AI Act tells you to document
      compliance. ISO/IEC 42001 tells you to build a management system. None of
      them tell you whether your system can actually be stopped when it's
      harming someone.
    </p>
    <p>
      Those frameworks improve documentation, oversight, and accountability.
      They are useful. But in high-stakes systems, the dominant failure mode is
      now temporal: decisions occur at machine speed while reversal occurs at
      institutional speed. In that gap, harm compounds.
    </p>
    <p>
      Ethotechnics treats governability as an operational capability — not a
      compliance posture. It asks whether a running system can be halted,
      reversed, and repaired under stress, on a clock that matters to the person
      being harmed.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/standards/#comparison">See the full standards comparison →</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-sm card">
    <p class="eyebrow">The governance landscape</p>
    <h2>Where Ethotechnics fits</h2>
    <p>
      AI governance is a growing market. Enterprises spend on compliance
      platforms, evaluation tools, and guardrail systems. These products build
      governance features into software.
    </p>
    <p>
      Ethotechnics is not a product. It is the open standard that products can
      measure against. The Institute publishes benchmarks, test cases, and
      versioned standards. Vendors build tools that implement those standards.
      Organizations use both to demonstrate governance to auditors, regulators,
      and affected people.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Institute provides the open standards. The Studio provides the
      <a
        href="https://ethotechnics.com/studio/"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        target="_blank">certified evaluations</a
      >.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-sm card">
    <p class="eyebrow">Citing this work</p>
    <h2>How to cite Ethotechnics</h2>
    <p>
      Ethotechnics Institute materials are published under
      <a
        href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        target="_blank"
      >
        CC BY 4.0
      </a>. To cite the framework:
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      Jain, K. (2025). <em
        >Ethotechnics: A governance and care-infrastructure framework for
        high-stakes systems.</em
      >
      Ethotechnics Institute. https://ethotechnics.org
    </blockquote>
    <p class="small muted">
      Individual glossary entries and patterns include their own citation
      metadata.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
